4. Permitted Evaluation and Collaboration Uses

You may:

1. View and analyze the source code.
2. Download and clone the Official Repository.
3. Download, clone, run, test, and modify an Authorized Fork.
4. Keep local copies for Evaluation.
5. Install dependencies required for Evaluation.
6. Run the Software in a local or isolated non-production Evaluation
   Environment.
7. Run Quick Start flows, tests, benchmarks, examples, demos, proof paths, and
   documented evaluation workloads.
8. Make private modifications solely for analysis, testing, debugging,
   integration assessment, or preparation of a Code Contribution or
   Documentation Contribution.
9. Create test integrations, tools, skills, agents, application hosts,
   plugins, and adapters solely for Evaluation and collaboration.
10. Compare branches, review a proposed patch, and collaborate on a pull
    request.
11. Publish technical observations, test reports, and descriptions of system
    behavior arising from permitted Evaluation.
12. Open issues, discussions, and pull requests.

The rights in this Section 4 apply only for Evaluation, code review,
technical collaboration, and preparation or review of a Code Contribution or
Documentation Contribution.

Labeling a real deployment as a "pilot", "test", "sandbox", or "proof of
concept" does not change its character if the system supports real business
processes, uses production data, serves customers or operational users, or
replaces a production tool. Labeling production data, customer-facing
services, or operational deployments as a "test" does not convert Production
Use or Commercial Use into Evaluation.

5. Forks, Patches, and Contributions

You may create an Authorized Fork, maintain that fork for Evaluation,
discussion, and preparation of a Code Contribution or Documentation
Contribution, publish patches and diffs, submit a pull request to the
Official Repository, and share code excerpts reasonably necessary to discuss a
bug, Code Contribution, or Documentation Contribution.

A contributor may share an Authorized Fork. Other evaluators may clone and test
an Authorized Fork for permitted Evaluation and collaboration. An Authorized
Fork does not become an independent distribution. Rights to an Authorized Fork
do not include Production Use or Commercial Use. The owner of an Authorized
Fork may not grant broader rights to Licensed Materials than those granted by
this license.

An Authorized Fork may not be marketed as an independent distribution of
Intergrax, sold, sublicensed, offered as a hosted service, or used for
Production Use or Commercial Use without separate explicit written permission
from the Licensor.

6. Restrictions

Except where such restriction is prohibited by applicable law, and except as
expressly permitted above, without separate explicit written permission from
the Licensor you may not:

- Use the Software for Production Use or Commercial Use.
- Offer a paid or free service operated with or on top of Intergrax.
- Provide SaaS, hosting, or service-bureau use of the Software.
- Sell, sublicense, or commercially distribute the Software.
- Incorporate the Software into another product or service.
- Publish an independent modified distribution of the Software.
- Offer, market, distribute, or provide to third parties a standalone or
  hosted platform based substantially on the Software that is presented as a
  substitute for the Intergrax Harness AI / Agent OS platform.
- Remove copyright notices or license information.
- Represent the project as your own.
- Use the Intergrax name or marks in a way that suggests official partnership
  or endorsement.
- Circumvent these restrictions by providing access to the Software to third
  parties outside permitted Evaluation and collaboration.

Nothing in this Section 6 limits your right to study, analyze, and understand
the publicly available source code for permitted Evaluation, to build original
code that does not contain Intergrax, or to conduct private evaluation,
prototyping, comparison benchmarks, or independent research and development on
similar problems. All Production Use, Commercial Use, hosting, and
incorporation into products or services still require separate explicit
written permission regardless of this clause.

